{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Njch5BqQZMnHfLpS3dkuoK","type":"tool_result","content":"210\t    # requires CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE=PLAN-156-COUNCIL-GUARD-EXTENSION AND\n211\t    # CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE_ACK=I-ACCEPT in addition to SENT-GK-F.\n212\t    #\n213\t    # PLAN-156-FOLLOWUP F3 (S270 live-fire finding, OQ1 Owner-ratified):\n214\t    # the exact-path entry guarded the INSTANCE, not the CLASS. A sibling\n215\t    # (or nested) workflow could carry the same external-lane egress and be\n216\t    # ordinary-writable — \"a file we choose not to ship is exactly the file\n217\t    # an attacker would CREATE\". The glob covers subdirectories too; `**`\n218\t    # matches zero or more segments in _fnmatch_segments (Sprint 9).\n219\t    # Cost accepted at ratification: authoring ANY .claude/workflows/*.js\n220\t    # becomes a sentinel ceremony.\n221\t    \".claude/workflows/**/*.js\",\n222\t    \".claude/commands/council.md\",\n223\t    # PLAN-165 p1-corrected (CX-1, S290): the night-mode posture-write\n224\t    # surface. The per-tool deny entries in settings.json close the\n225\t    # Edit/Write rail; THESE entries are the load-bearing rail for Bash —\n226\t    # check_bash_safety keys write-shaped (and -c/-e-body) command\n227\t    # references off this list, so without them\n228\t    # `echo '{...}' > settings.local.json` passes under acceptEdits with\n229\t    # the deny applied (the codex CX-1 refutation of the original p1).\n230\t    # Listing the WRITER script here also removes model-rail invocation\n231\t    # of the toggle (OQ1-redo, Owner-ratified 2026-08-03: presence =\n232\t    # only a human at the keyboard runs on/off, via `!` or a terminal).\n233\t    \".claude/settings.local.json\",\n234\t    \".claude/state/night-mode.json\",\n235\t    \".claude/scripts/night-mode.py\",\n236\t    # PLAN162_FIX_3 (PLAN-162 findings #3 + #8, consensus C10) — guard the\n237\t    # files the guard itself TRUSTS. Both were guarded by NEITHER rail on\n238\t    # HEAD (verified: canonical=False AND kernel=False), which is what\n239\t    # separates them from finding #12 (that one at least has the kernel\n240\t    # hard-deny underneath).\n241\t    #\n242\t    #   1. `.claude/security/sentinel-signers-registry.yaml` — the ADR-121\n243\t    #      identity root read below in `_sentinel_grants_path`. An ordinary\n244\t    #      Edit silently disarmed half the dual signer rail.\n245\t    #   2. `.claude/policies/.drift-manifest.json` — the policy hash-pin\n246\t    #      registry. Rewritable without the sentinel it pins; the parallel\n247\t    #      of #3 (\"pin the pinner\").\n248\t    #\n249\t    # BOTH are ALSO in `_KERNEL_PATHS` (same wave) — the sentinel rail"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"44c9ec98-ac5f-45ac-a8b8-721313d99788","uuid":"064cf9d4-c918-4fd6-8a33-b4049296986b","timestamp":"2026-11-16T18:40:11.040Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.n5R3O9/tree/.claude/hooks/_lib/canonical_edit/guards.py","content":"    # requires CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE=PLAN-156-COUNCIL-GUARD-EXTENSION AND\n    # CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE_ACK=I-ACCEPT in addition to SENT-GK-F.\n    #\n    # PLAN-156-FOLLOWUP F3 (S270 live-fire finding, OQ1 Owner-ratified):\n    # the exact-path entry guarded the INSTANCE, not the CLASS. A sibling\n    # (or nested) workflow could carry the same external-lane egress and be\n    # ordinary-writable — \"a file we choose not to ship is exactly the file\n    # an attacker would CREATE\". The glob covers subdirectories too; `**`\n    # matches zero or more segments in _fnmatch_segments (Sprint 9).\n    # Cost accepted at ratification: authoring ANY .claude/workflows/*.js\n    # becomes a sentinel ceremony.\n    \".claude/workflows/**/*.js\",\n    \".claude/commands/council.md\",\n    # PLAN-165 p1-corrected (CX-1, S290): the night-mode posture-write\n    # surface. The per-tool deny entries in settings.json close the\n    # Edit/Write rail; THESE entries are the load-bearing rail for Bash —\n    # check_bash_safety keys write-shaped (and -c/-e-body) command\n    # references off this list, so without them\n    # `echo '{...}' > settings.local.json` passes under acceptEdits with\n    # the deny applied (the codex CX-1 refutation of the original p1).\n    # Listing the WRITER script here also removes model-rail invocation\n    # of the toggle (OQ1-redo, Owner-ratified 2026-08-03: presence =\n    # only a human at the keyboard runs on/off, via `!` or a terminal).\n    \".claude/settings.local.json\",\n    \".claude/state/night-mode.json\",\n    \".claude/scripts/night-mode.py\",\n    # PLAN162_FIX_3 (PLAN-162 findings #3 + #8, consensus C10) — guard the\n    # files the guard itself TRUSTS. Both were guarded by NEITHER rail on\n    # HEAD (verified: canonical=False AND kernel=False), which is what\n    # separates them from finding #12 (that one at least has the kernel\n    # hard-deny underneath).\n    #\n    #   1. `.claude/security/sentinel-signers-registry.yaml` — the ADR-121\n    #      identity root read below in `_sentinel_grants_path`. An ordinary\n    #      Edit silently disarmed half the dual signer rail.\n    #   2. `.claude/policies/.drift-manifest.json` — the policy hash-pin\n    #      registry. Rewritable without the sentinel it pins; the parallel\n    #      of #3 (\"pin the pinner\").\n    #\n    # BOTH are ALSO in `_KERNEL_PATHS` (same wave) — the sentinel rail","numLines":40,"startLine":210,"totalLines":387}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Njch5BqQZMnHfLpS3dkuoK","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch76VNcxW33jZ9jphJzgb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CAkvfM7Wtb8h6SfdscJE6Y","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.n5R3O9/tree/.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/posture_tables.py","offset":290,"limit":95},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2883,"cache_read_input_tokens":188116,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2883},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"44c9ec98-ac5f-45ac-a8b8-721313d99788","uuid":"5d397c5f-b049-407b-b02b-d194ce1acffe","timestamp":"2026-11-16T18:40:13.122Z","request_id":"req_011Ch76VNQZTL1orN9PHUa7w","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01CAkvfM7Wtb8h6SfdscJE6Y":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.n5R3O9/tree/.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/posture_tables.py","offset":290,"limit":95}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CAkvfM7Wtb8h6SfdscJE6Y","type":"tool_result","content":"290\t# PLAN-165 NF-08 — posture-toggle INVOCATION guard (round-3 security review)\n291\t# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n292\t#\n293\t# `_e3_check_canonical_path_write` above matches WRITE shapes only. A canonical\n294\t# path sitting in the SCRIPT position of an interpreter is not one of them, so\n295\t#\n296\t#     python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.py on\n297\t#     ./.claude/scripts/night-mode.py on\n298\t#\n299\t# both returned ALLOW while three signed comments (check_canonical_edit.py's\n300\t# `_CANONICAL_GUARDS` note, settings.json's `_night_mode_deny_surface_comment`,\n301\t# and the round-3 sentinel) claimed the model rail could not invoke the toggle.\n302\t# Listing the writer in `_CANONICAL_GUARDS` protects the FILE; this matcher\n303\t# protects the ACTION.\n304\t#\n305\t# Property being PURSUED — Owner ratification OQ1-redo (2026-08-03): arming or\n306\t# disarming the autonomy posture is a HUMAN action (the `!` prefix or a\n307\t# terminal). This matcher raises the cost of the model rail doing it; it does\n308\t# NOT close the boundary, and saying otherwise would be a false claim on a\n309\t# signed surface (codex S292 r8 P1). Static parsing of bash is incomplete by\n310\t# construction: a RENAMED INTERPRETER (`./zz-interp <toggle> on`) passes both\n311\t# this matcher and the script's self-path guard — measured, see\n312\t# nf08-invocation-guard-NOTES.md §7 residual 5. The perimeter that actually\n313\t# holds is the CURRENT session's defaultMode + Bash allowlist; this rail is\n314\t# defence-in-depth plus the pedagogical message pointing at `!`.\n315\t#\n316\t# DIVISION OF LABOUR with `_e3` (asserted from both sides by\n317\t# `.claude/hooks/tests/test_bash_posture_toggle_invocation.py`):\n318\t#   • `_e3` blob-scan → INDIRECT bodies (`bash -c '…'`, `eval`, `xargs`,\n319\t#     `find -exec`) already deny, because the toggle path is a literal\n320\t#     `_CANONICAL_GUARDS` entry. Those keep the older canonical message.\n321\t#   • `_e4` (here)    → DIRECT argv forms: the path as the command word, or as\n322\t#     the operand an interpreter/prefix runner would execute, plus the\n323\t#     CREATION of an executable alias (`ln`, see `_E4_LINK_RUNNERS`).\n324\t#   • `night-mode.py`'s own SELF-PATH GUARD → execution through an alias that\n325\t#     already exists. A matcher sees spellings; an alias exists precisely to\n326\t#     have a different one, so this layer is the boundary and the two above\n327\t#     are the early signal. See the r2 note at the end of this comment.\n328\t#\n329\t# Scope note: this denies EVERY invocation, `status` included. Carving out a\n330\t# read-only subcommand would key a PERMISSIVE branch off argv parsing — the\n331\t# exact shape the fail-closed doctrine rejects — and the recovery route (`!`)\n332\t# is always available, so a blanket deny strands nobody.\n333\t#\n334\t# CODEX S292 REVIEW (two P1 bypasses of the FIRST implementation, both fixed\n335\t# below and pinned red-first in the test file):\n336\t#   P1-A  a raw-substring pre-filter returned ALLOW before shlex ever ran, so\n337\t#         any spelling the SHELL normalises escaped: `night\"-\"mode.py`,\n338\t#         `night''-mode.py`, `night\\-mode.py`, and — on APFS — `NIGHT-MODE.PY`.\n339\t#         Fix: no pre-filter (always tokenize), and compare CASE-FOLDED.\n340\t#         Folded in beyond the finding: glob spellings (`night-mod?.py`).\n341\t#   P1-B  a value-bearing flag on a prefix runner (`env -u FOO python3 …`,\n342\t#         `sudo -u root python3 …`) had its VALUE read as the command word,\n343\t#         and the scan stopped there — the interpreter and the script that\n344\t#         followed were never examined. Fix: resolve the whole runner chain\n345\t#         with a per-runner flag table, and treat an UNKNOWN flag as\n346\t#         ambiguous (fail-closed) rather than as boolean.\n347\t# Both were found on the model's own draft, by the cross-vendor pair rail.\n348\t#\n349\t# CODEX S292 REVIEW ROUND 2 (P1 — executable ALIASES). The fixed matcher still\n350\t# recognised only SPELLINGS, so `ln -s .claude/scripts/night-mode.py /tmp/nm &&\n351\t# python3 /tmp/nm on` armed the posture: `ln` reads the toggle (not a write, so\n352\t# `_e3` allows it), and at execution time nothing but `/tmp/nm` is on the\n353\t# command line. Measured on this platform before fixing — the alias printed\n354\t# `result=applied` and wrote `defaultMode: acceptEdits`. Re-probing widened the\n355\t# class beyond the finding: a RENAMED COPY inside the repo\n356\t# (`cp …/night-mode.py .claude/scripts/nm2.py && python3 .claude/scripts/nm2.py\n357\t# on`) armed it too, and no `ln` rule would have caught that one.\n358\t#\n359\t# The fix is therefore split by what each layer can actually know:\n360\t#   • HERE — deny creating a link that names the toggle (`_E4_LINK_RUNNERS`).\n361\t#     Cheap, string-only, and the earliest signal. NOT sufficient: the alias\n362\t#     can also arrive from a previous session, a `git checkout`, `os.symlink`,\n363\t#     `tar -x`, or the operator's own hand.\n364\t#   • IN THE SCRIPT — `night-mode.py` refuses to run unless the file it was\n365\t#     invoked as IS the canonical `<repo>/.claude/scripts/night-mode.py`. That\n366\t#     covers both members of the class, holds however the alias was made, and\n367\t#     holds even if this hook never runs.\n368\t# Doing the execution half here was considered and REJECTED: it would require\n369\t# resolving an attacker-supplied path on every Bash command (FS syscall in the\n370\t# hook budget, `RuntimeError` on a symlink loop — the fail-open class the S277\n371\t# pair rail found in this repo's own draft — and a TOCTOU window).\n372\t\n373\t# The governance surface. One entry today; the frozenset is the seam for any\n374\t# future posture writer (add the path here AND to `_CANONICAL_GUARDS` — the\n375\t# coherence test in test_bash_posture_toggle_invocation.py fails otherwise).\n376\t_E4_POSTURE_TOGGLE_SCRIPTS = frozenset({\".claude/scripts/night-mode.py\"})\n377\t_E4_TOGGLE_BASENAMES = frozenset(\n378\t    _e3_basename(_p) for _p in _E4_POSTURE_TOGGLE_SCRIPTS\n379\t)\n380\t\n381\t# Case-folded twins. Codex S292 P1-A: on a case-INSENSITIVE filesystem — APFS,\n382\t# this repo's default platform — `.claude/scripts/NIGHT-MODE.PY` IS the toggle,\n383\t# and an exact-case comparison waved it through. Same defect class, same fix,\n384\t# and deliberately the same spelling as the write rail's"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"44c9ec98-ac5f-45ac-a8b8-721313d99788","uuid":"3bcb6f05-e36a-4046-aaf8-906c4f2c5a49","timestamp":"2026-11-16T18:40:13.142Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.n5R3O9/tree/.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/posture_tables.py","content":"# PLAN-165 NF-08 — posture-toggle INVOCATION guard (round-3 security review)\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n#\n# `_e3_check_canonical_path_write` above matches WRITE shapes only. A canonical\n# path sitting in the SCRIPT position of an interpreter is not one of them, so\n#\n#     python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.py on\n#     ./.claude/scripts/night-mode.py on\n#\n# both returned ALLOW while three signed comments (check_canonical_edit.py's\n# `_CANONICAL_GUARDS` note, settings.json's `_night_mode_deny_surface_comment`,\n# and the round-3 sentinel) claimed the model rail could not invoke the toggle.\n# Listing the writer in `_CANONICAL_GUARDS` protects the FILE; this matcher\n# protects the ACTION.\n#\n# Property being PURSUED — Owner ratification OQ1-redo (2026-08-03): arming or\n# disarming the autonomy posture is a HUMAN action (the `!` prefix or a\n# terminal). This matcher raises the cost of the model rail doing it; it does\n# NOT close the boundary, and saying otherwise would be a false claim on a\n# signed surface (codex S292 r8 P1). Static parsing of bash is incomplete by\n# construction: a RENAMED INTERPRETER (`./zz-interp <toggle> on`) passes both\n# this matcher and the script's self-path guard — measured, see\n# nf08-invocation-guard-NOTES.md §7 residual 5. The perimeter that actually\n# holds is the CURRENT session's defaultMode + Bash allowlist; this rail is\n# defence-in-depth plus the pedagogical message pointing at `!`.\n#\n# DIVISION OF LABOUR with `_e3` (asserted from both sides by\n# `.claude/hooks/tests/test_bash_posture_toggle_invocation.py`):\n#   • `_e3` blob-scan → INDIRECT bodies (`bash -c '…'`, `eval`, `xargs`,\n#     `find -exec`) already deny, because the toggle path is a literal\n#     `_CANONICAL_GUARDS` entry. Those keep the older canonical message.\n#   • `_e4` (here)    → DIRECT argv forms: the path as the command word, or as\n#     the operand an interpreter/prefix runner would execute, plus the\n#     CREATION of an executable alias (`ln`, see `_E4_LINK_RUNNERS`).\n#   • `night-mode.py`'s own SELF-PATH GUARD → execution through an alias that\n#     already exists. A matcher sees spellings; an alias exists precisely to\n#     have a different one, so this layer is the boundary and the two above\n#     are the early signal. See the r2 note at the end of this comment.\n#\n# Scope note: this denies EVERY invocation, `status` included. Carving out a\n# read-only subcommand would key a PERMISSIVE branch off argv parsing — the\n# exact shape the fail-closed doctrine rejects — and the recovery route (`!`)\n# is always available, so a blanket deny strands nobody.\n#\n# CODEX S292 REVIEW (two P1 bypasses of the FIRST implementation, both fixed\n# below and pinned red-first in the test file):\n#   P1-A  a raw-substring pre-filter returned ALLOW before shlex ever ran, so\n#         any spelling the SHELL normalises escaped: `night\"-\"mode.py`,\n#         `night''-mode.py`, `night\\-mode.py`, and — on APFS — `NIGHT-MODE.PY`.\n#         Fix: no pre-filter (always tokenize), and compare CASE-FOLDED.\n#         Folded in beyond the finding: glob spellings (`night-mod?.py`).\n#   P1-B  a value-bearing flag on a prefix runner (`env -u FOO python3 …`,\n#         `sudo -u root python3 …`) had its VALUE read as the command word,\n#         and the scan stopped there — the interpreter and the script that\n#         followed were never examined. Fix: resolve the whole runner chain\n#         with a per-runner flag table, and treat an UNKNOWN flag as\n#         ambiguous (fail-closed) rather than as boolean.\n# Both were found on the model's own draft, by the cross-vendor pair rail.\n#\n# CODEX S292 REVIEW ROUND 2 (P1 — executable ALIASES). The fixed matcher still\n# recognised only SPELLINGS, so `ln -s .claude/scripts/night-mode.py /tmp/nm &&\n# python3 /tmp/nm on` armed the posture: `ln` reads the toggle (not a write, so\n# `_e3` allows it), and at execution time nothing but `/tmp/nm` is on the\n# command line. Measured on this platform before fixing — the alias printed\n# `result=applied` and wrote `defaultMode: acceptEdits`. Re-probing widened the\n# class beyond the finding: a RENAMED COPY inside the repo\n# (`cp …/night-mode.py .claude/scripts/nm2.py && python3 .claude/scripts/nm2.py\n# on`) armed it too, and no `ln` rule would have caught that one.\n#\n# The fix is therefore split by what each layer can actually know:\n#   • HERE — deny creating a link that names the toggle (`_E4_LINK_RUNNERS`).\n#     Cheap, string-only, and the earliest signal. NOT sufficient: the alias\n#     can also arrive from a previous session, a `git checkout`, `os.symlink`,\n#     `tar -x`, or the operator's own hand.\n#   • IN THE SCRIPT — `night-mode.py` refuses to run unless the file it was\n#     invoked as IS the canonical `<repo>/.claude/scripts/night-mode.py`. That\n#     covers both members of the class, holds however the alias was made, and\n#     holds even if this hook never runs.\n# Doing the execution half here was considered and REJECTED: it would require\n# resolving an attacker-supplied path on every Bash command (FS syscall in the\n# hook budget, `RuntimeError` on a symlink loop — the fail-open class the S277\n# pair rail found in this repo's own draft — and a TOCTOU window).\n\n# The governance surface. One entry today; the frozenset is the seam for any\n# future posture writer (add the path here AND to `_CANONICAL_GUARDS` — the\n# coherence test in test_bash_posture_toggle_invocation.py fails otherwise).\n_E4_POSTURE_TOGGLE_SCRIPTS = frozenset({\".claude/scripts/night-mode.py\"})\n_E4_TOGGLE_BASENAMES = frozenset(\n    _e3_basename(_p) for _p in _E4_POSTURE_TOGGLE_SCRIPTS\n)\n\n# Case-folded twins. Codex S292 P1-A: on a case-INSENSITIVE filesystem — APFS,\n# this repo's default platform — `.claude/scripts/NIGHT-MODE.PY` IS the toggle,\n# and an exact-case comparison waved it through. Same defect class, same fix,\n# and deliberately the same spelling as the write rail's","numLines":95,"startLine":290,"totalLines":536}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CAkvfM7Wtb8h6SfdscJE6Y","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
