{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch76hkuEXUMgHbC62RMGm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Decision: GO.** Payload 11 makes none of the declared conditions false, and I found no P0 and no new P1. There are eight P2 follow-ups below. This was a static review only: I had read and search tools and ran no code.\n\n**What holds up**\n- **The moved code is wired in correctly.** All four entry points pull their moved code into their own namespace: `check_pair_rail.py`, `_lib/policy.py`, `.claude/scripts/lessons.py` and `.claude/scripts/night-mode.py`.\n  - Patches, `global` writes and `__file__`-based paths all act on the entry point, not on the new modules.\n  - The pin-check exception `CodexPinMismatch` is the same object where it is raised and where it is caught.\n  - The only name a moved module imports but does not hand over, `_cli_currency`, is imported by the entry point itself (`check_pair_rail.py:21`).\n- **Failure directions are correct.**\n  - If `check_pair_rail.py` fails to import, Python exits 1 with nothing on stdout. `_python-hook.sh:412-416` passes that code through, so the session is not blocked (fails open).\n  - A failed import of `_lib.policy` is caught at `policy_dispatch.py:45-49`.\n  - A night-mode failure writes nothing.\n- **Protection and shipping.**\n  - `_policy_parts` and `_pair_rail_parts` are on the kernel list (`check_arbitration_kernel.py:311`, `:315`).\n  - The lessons and night-mode modules are covered by the canonical guard `.claude/hooks/_lib/**/*.py`.\n  - The installer ships all four new packages.\n- **Standard library only, Python 3.9 compatible.**\n- **Adopters are not affected by the Codex pin change.** Installs do not receive `.claude/governance/`, so the pin check finds no manifest and stays advisory (CHANGELOG lines 204-206).\n- **Conditions 9 and 10 match the code.** A Codex newer than the manifest version (0.160.0) and at or above the floor passes as `compatible`, and `--verify-codex-pin` exits 0 for both `verified` and `compatible`.\n- **The P1 Claude filed on payload 10 is still there.** When the host's triple is missing from the manifest, no sha256 is computed (`part_01.py:477-486`). It is already in the signed annex, so it is not counted as new here.\n\n**P2 follow-ups**\n1. **A vanished override is no longer blocked on most hosts** (`part_01.py:477-486` against `:506-521`).\n   - On a host whose triple is not in the manifest, the version check runs before the \"override file must exist\" check.\n   - So `CEO_PAIR_RAIL_CODEX_BIN` pointing at a missing path, under a folder with a qualifying `@openai/codex` `package.json`, passes as `compatible`. The run then fails and the rail drops to advisory. The base blocked this case.\n   - This breaks the comment's own rule (\"inexistente = BLOQUEIA\"). Practical impact is low, because anyone who can set that variable can already use the kill switch.\n   - Fix: require `is_file()` on the override before applying the version policy.\n2. **Pin comments no longer match the code.**\n   - `part_01.py:408-409` says \"No env relaxes this comparison\", and `:412` leaves `compatible` out of the status list.\n   - `part_01.py:575` says it returns the native payload, but it returns the launcher on the missing-triple route.\n   - `part_04.py:499` says exit 0 means the sha256 matched.\n   - `part_02.py:66` says the hashed file is the file that runs.\n   - Fix: update these comments.\n3. **The recorded Codex version can differ from the one that passed** (`part_01.py:557-570`).\n   - `installed_version` comes from the first `@openai/codex` `package.json` found. The pass decision can come from a different one, further up the tree.\n   - So a result can read `installed_version=0.150.0` with `status=compatible`.\n   - Fix: record the version that `compatible_package_version` returns, and only consider the nearest qualifying file.\n4. **No audit trail for a version-only pass** (`part_01.py:583-584`). The live hook accepts `compatible` silently, so nothing distinguishes a hash-verified run from a version-only one. Fix: emit an audit event carrying `version_floor_only`.\n5. **Dead duplicate of `_invoke_codex_review`.**\n   - `check_pair_rail.py:29-210` redefines it after the install step, so the copy in `part_02.py` never runs.\n   - Condition 7 tells users to patch the module, so a hand-patch there would be silently ignored.\n   - Fix: keep one copy.\n6. **The policy module loses its docstring** (`_policy_parts/__init__.py:15`).\n   - Executing each part overwrites `_lib.policy.__doc__` with \"Extracted policy declarations: loading.\".\n   - `inspect.getsource` can no longer find classes such as `Policy`.\n   - Fix: restore `__doc__` after the install, or remove the part docstrings.\n7. **The night-mode toggle code can now be imported without its guards** (`night-mode.py:327-331` and `_night_mode_parts/toggles.py`).\n   - `cmd_on` and `cmd_off` can be called from `_lib._night_mode_parts` without the self-path check, and without the command line naming any guarded path. From reading the code (I did not run it), a `python3 -c` body could arm `acceptEdits` this way without tripping the Bash guards.\n   - It stays P2: the code's own comments say the guard does not close this boundary (`posture_tables.py:305-314`), and splitting the path string to write the overlay directly was already just as easy.\n   - Fix: run the self-path check inside `cmd_on` and `cmd_off`.\n8. **A relocated night-mode copy now crashes instead of refusing** (`night-mode.py:330`).\n   - The parts import now runs before the self-path check (`:518`). A relocated copy without `_lib` beside it crashes (exit 1, no summary line or audit record) instead of the documented refusal (exit 2 with both records, docstring `:207`).\n   - It still writes nothing.\n   - Fix: run the self-path check first, or guard the import.\n\n**New findings (annex):** none at P1."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":n